{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact head 676de561be82e2a0106d2ee4fbee39bcb5d96fcb, reviewed in a detached worktree at that commit with a clean tree.\n\nREPRODUCED MEASUREMENTS. I re-measured independently on my own freshly prepared linear / one-actor / fan-out-one fixture (logical digest fb44f3592aae22494a4fcef71641b10fd1de010466f42fef9ce0e972f38321de), cold_status, tail -1, two alternating rounds per revision, peak RSS read from getrusage(RUSAGE_SELF).Maxrss as the report measured it.\n\nDepth | base 394fcd56 r1 / r2 | candidate r1 / r2\n500 | 50,298,880 / 50,577,408 | 49,479,680 / 49,020,928\n5,000 | 223,002,624 / 220,086,272 | 135,528,448 / 164,265,984\n50,000 | 1,930,592,256 / 1,919,614,976 | 925,679,616 / 927,268,864\n\nEvery point lands within about one percent of the reported figures except one 5,000-record candidate sample that ran 21 percent high, still far below base. Measured slope over the two decades 500 to 50,000 is 37,758 to 37,986 B/record on base and 17,701 to 17,742 B/record on the candidate, against the 37,764 and 17,868 in the report. Both 50,000-record candidate runs sit under 1 GiB at 0.862 and 0.864 GiB, a 13.6 percent margin, cutting peak RSS 51.7 percent against base. Parent request 6dd1fbcf is satisfied on its own terms: the slope is established by measurement across two decades of depth, and 50,000 records serve under 1 GiB. The base slope also settles the open question in that request, whose evidence gave a range of 36.8 to 63.7 KB/record from cross-scenario comparisons; the clean same-fixture number is 37.8 KB/record, so the low end was the right one.\n\nLINEAR, NOT ASYMPTOTICALLY BOUNDED. Retention is still linear in depth at roughly half the constant, and the artifacts should not be read as claiming otherwise. checkpointEventCache.chunks grows one 4,096-event gzip chunk per 4,096 records and is never released; only the raw tail is bounded. Extrapolating the measured candidate slope, 1 GiB is crossed near 58,000 records, about 16 percent of headroom above the 50,000 the request names. The implementation report and performance/BASELINE.md are honest about this, both stating a per-record slope rather than a bound. The phrase \"retention is bounded\" in the kernel artifact is the loosest statement in the set and its own mechanism clause, \"plus a bounded raw tail\", disambiguates it. Not a blocking defect, but the next depth request should start from linear-with-a-smaller-constant rather than from \"bounded\".\n\nKERNEL CHECKPOINT RETENTION. Per-chunk RAM is O(chunk), not O(set). append seals at exactly 4,096 events into one gzip buffer, appends the compressed bytes, then clears the tail before truncating it; that clear is load-bearing, releasing every payload and attachment reference while reusing a 4,096-slot backing array, and I proved the release directly with a temporary test asserting the sealed slots hold nil Payload and Attachments. On write, marshalCompactCheckpoint streams each chunk back through a gzip reader into the outer writer with io.Copy, so no chunk set is ever materialized at once, and checkpointLimitWriter still bounds the compressed output during that copy. checkpointMaterial clones payload and attachment bytes, so the cache never aliases kernel events.\n\nThe signed on-disk format does not change, and I tested this rather than reasoned about it. Cached, CachedChunks and CachedTail are all json:\"-\" and reach nothing but the in-process marshal. A temporary test compared marshalCheckpoint over the cached path against the identical event set marshalled the old way at counts 0, 1, 4095, 4096, 4097, 8191, 8192 and 8193, covering every chunk boundary: the container bytes are byte-identical at every count and decode back to the exact payloads and attachments. This is checkpoint@3 unchanged, not a checkpoint@4 and not an extension. Schema constant, manifest fields and canonical-JSON check untouched, single gzip payload, cadence still 256.\n\nAPPLICATION FOLD RETENTION. Nothing can need a cleared event afterward. loaded.Events comes from Reader.load under r.mu; the cache nils log.Events before storing it, so the kernel keeps no reference, and every hand-out is a fresh slice, the cache-hit path returning no events at all. snapshotWithSource holds w.snapshotMu throughout and w.reader is per-Workspace. After the loop the function reads only loaded.Verification, and the delta branch does not clear. On an error or panic mid-consumption, loaded is a discarded local; Folder.Append returns no error and records an ineffective decision rather than failing, so no partial-consumption path re-reads. The fold already nils Record.Payload and Attachments after Decode, which is exactly why clearing the transport slot is what actually frees the bytes.\n\nThe rewrite from newFolder(records) to newFolder(nil) plus per-record Append is exactly equivalent, and this deserved checking rather than assuming: NewFolder indexes by loop position while Append indexes by len(state.records). They agree only because every path through foldState.append reaches addDecision exactly once and addDecision always appends one entry. It does; I walked all six returns. That matters because the fold has genuine index-sensitive behaviour, the genesis roster being recognised only at index 0. The observer Items change from len(records) to Verification.Events is equal on this path, since scanHead increments Verification.Events in lockstep with every Events append and the checkpoint path sets one from the other.\n\nPARITY. Decisive and clean. I built gs from main 394fcd56 and from the head, ran each against its own copy of the live 6,142-record repository, and the two status --json outputs are byte-identical: sha256 31f4bda346f445e9470b24c925f9b118c8c62f1aa0cbb63f2fbe68d65f156579 over 13,347,610 bytes from both. On that same live log the head cut maximum resident set size from 182,976,512 to 136,183,808 B and peak footprint from 160,678,608 to 103,744,208 B, a 25.6 percent reduction on real data. The live resident at depth 6,142 on the pre-change binary holds 307.6 MB RSS and still answers /v0/status in 0.03 s.\n\nMUTATIONS. Off-by-one at the chunk boundary, sealing at 4,097 instead of 4,096: caught loudly by the head's own new TestCheckpointEventCacheCompressesDepthAndRoundTrips and by both of my temporary tests, and the count invariant in marshalCompactCheckpoint would independently refuse to write such a checkpoint. Restored and verified clean.\n\nBreaking the clearing, by deleting the single line that releases each transport record, is the finding worth carrying forward. Nothing goes red: internal/app, internal/kernel, internal/service and internal/perfscenario are all green with it removed. Yet 50,000 records then peak at 1,295,777,792 B, 1.207 GiB, past the bound by 21 percent. The two halves of the change are each necessary: kernel chunking takes 1.919 GiB to 1.207, and the app clearing takes 1.207 to 0.864. So one line carrying a third of the win can be deleted with every suite still green. That is not a defect in this head and the parent request did not ask for a guard, so it does not block approval; I recommend a bounded child request for a bounding regression the suite can actually run, with the number above as its scoping evidence. Restored and verified clean.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layers from docs/reference/architecture.md are internal/kernel (Kernel) and internal/app (Application host and boundary adapter). Both contracts are preserved and no page needs updating in this head. The kernel row's claim that a checkpoint caches only kernel-verified event material and kernel identity, never projection state or an application profile, still holds: chunking changes the in-process representation of that material and nothing about what is cached. The stated schema stays gitseq-checkpoint@3 with @1 and @2 read compatibility, which the byte-identity test confirms rather than assumes. The app row's ownership of the checkpoint pointer, the profile-gated projection cache and snapshotFlight is untouched, and the parity result is the evidence that projection ownership did not move. performance/BASELINE.md is the right home for the retained point measurements and keeps the project's stated posture of not promoting timings into guarantees.\n\nSECURITY. No weakening of verification to save memory, examined from both ends. The trust boundary is where it was: material enters the cache only after kernel verification, the signing and self-verify in publishCheckpoint are the unmodified tail of the old writeCheckpoint, and the ref moves only after VerifySSHCommit succeeds against the current sequencer key. No untrusted input reaches the new code, since chunks are produced in-process from already-verified events and read back by the same process, so signature verification still precedes any decompression of untrusted material. Bounds hold: maxCheckpointBytes is still enforced through checkpointLimitWriter during the chunk copy, maxCheckpointManifest is unchanged, and the read-side per-event ceilings, attachment count and name limits, Multistream(false) and the trailing-byte checks are untouched. Failure modes fail closed: writeCheckpointCache refuses on a cache error or on the cached count disagreeing with Verification.Events, and a sealing error makes the cache sticky-failed until the next reset, degrading to counted checkpointFailures and a full rescan rather than writing anything unsound. Concurrency is unchanged, the Reader's cache under r.mu and the Submitter's under s.mu being separate instances, so no checkpointEventCache is shared; -race on kernel, app and service is green. One pre-existing observation, not introduced here and not blocking: decodeCompactCheckpoint pre-sizes from the manifest's own EventCount with an unbounded ceiling, but it is unreachable from the production read path, which goes through validateCompactCheckpoint and sizes from Git-derived positions.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. No doubled retention path was left behind: cloneEvent and cloneEvents are gone with no remaining callers, and checkpointEvents is now the single retention structure. The one removable indirection is writeCheckpointEvents, which has exactly one caller, writeCheckpoint, and re-checks options.enabled, SigningKey and the event count that its only caller just checked. Inlining it would leave the two real entry points, writeCheckpoint and writeCheckpointCache, sharing publishCheckpoint, which is the shape the change is already reaching for. Small enough that I am not requesting changes for it. Nothing else here is fluff: the chunk size, the clear before truncation, and the count invariant in marshalCompactCheckpoint all earn their place, and that invariant is what makes the boundary mutation impossible to write out silently.\n\nGATES at the exact head with a clean tree, my temporary tests removed first: go build ./... exit 0; go vet ./... exit 0; go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 across all 25 packages; go test -race on internal/kernel, internal/app and internal/service all ok.\n\nMERGE NOTE, not part of the verdict. All three artifacts carry live predecessors that step 5 must retire at merge: performance 1, internal/kernel 3, internal/app 7.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:d18976e834883b106011bd2b005dc0bc688b2d90","head":"676de561be82e2a0106d2ee4fbee39bcb5d96fcb","verdict":"approved"}}